<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dobrzyń Land</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ziemia dobrzyńska</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dobrzyń Land is a historical region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, north-east of Płock. A frontier land between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, it was repeatedly contested between Poland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, which held it from 1329 until the Treaty of Kalisz returned it to Poland in 1343, and again for a time around 1400 before Poland redeemed it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dobrzyn-land.jpg" alt="Dobrzyń Land" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1bumer · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%B3rzno,_ko%C5%9Bci%C3%B3%C5%82_Podwy%C5%BCszenia_Krzy%C5%BCa_%C5%9Awi%C4%99tego.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dobrzyń Land lies north-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drweca">Drwęca</a> and west of the Skrwa, and covers about 3,000 square kilometres, roughly the present-day powiats of Lipno, Rypin and part of Golub-Dobrzyń. Its historic capital, Dobrzyń nad Wisłą, gave the region its name, and its largest town is Rypin. It has many lakes and, as in Mazovia, many descendants of the petty nobility, so that palaces and manor houses are common. Its speech is usually grouped with that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a>, and it is seen as a transitional land between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>, Mazovia and Chełmno Land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region became part of the Polish state under Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>. On the death of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> in 1138 it went to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>, and in 1228 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I of Masovia</a> granted it to the Order of Dobrzyń, a body of German knights soon absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>. In the later 13th century it belonged to the Kuyavian Piasts, and with their lands it passed to the reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>. It was later part of the Inowrocław Voivodeship, though it remained in the Diocese of Płock.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a> of 1793. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Treaties of Tilsit</a> it joined the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in 1807, in 1815 it passed to Congress Poland under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and in 1918 to the Second Polish Republic; in 1920 Poland repulsed a Soviet invasion of the region. Under German occupation from 1939 its Polish people suffered mass arrests, slave labour, kidnapping of children, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> and killings, including the Intelligenzaktion, with massacres at Skrwilno, Rusinowo, Karnkowo and Rypin, until 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The region lies within the Kuyavian-Pomeranian Voivodeship and has about 200,000 inhabitants. Its oldest crowned sanctuary of Our Lady is at Skępe, the ruins of medieval castles stand at Złotoria and Bobrowniki, and the manor house at Szafarnia, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin">Fryderyk Chopin</a> spent the summers of 1824 and 1825, is a museum of the composer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dobrzyń Land” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Knights returned <b>Dobrzyń Land</b> and made only temporary concessions in Samogitia; the peace was not stable.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-1">1 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
